      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Some researchers have proposed subcultural theories, arguing that distinct value systems within specific ethnic communities may lead to higher aggregate rates of crime or substance use. In contrast, biological or genetic explanations are significantly less supported in modern criminology, though a few studies have attempted to measure heritability while controlling for environmental confounds, often yielding disputed or context-dependent results. It is important to note that mainstream social science overwhelmingly rejects biological determinism regarding human behavior, favoring instead explanations rooted in social structure, opportunity structures, and institutional bias. Therefore, accurately representing the \"other side\" requires highlighting these minority theoretical frameworks while contextualizing them as contested and often methodologically difficult to justify.",
